const canonical = "https://samedayappliance.repair/services/dishwasher-repair/bosch-not-draining/";

const title = "Bosch Dishwasher Not Draining: E24 & E25 — Same Day";
const description = "Bosch dishwasher won't drain, or showing E24 or E25? E24 is a blocked drain path, E25 is the drain pump. Filter and pump-cover checks, and why E15 is different. $89.";

const faqs = [
  {
    q: "Why is my Bosch dishwasher not draining?",
    a: "On a Bosch the standing water almost always traces to a clogged filter (the number-one cause), the drain pump or its cover, a kinked or clogged drain hose, a disposal knockout plug after a new disposal, or a blocked sink air gap. Bosch signals this with E24 (drain path blocked) or E25 (drain pump blocked). The filter, hose, disposal, air gap, and even a look at the pump impeller are homeowner-checkable per Bosch's own guidance; replacing the pump is the tech job. Start by twisting out and rinsing the filter. $89 residential diagnostic, waived with repair."
  },
  {
    q: "What is the difference between E24 and E25 on a Bosch dishwasher?",
    a: "Both are drain codes. E24 means the water isn't being pumped out — the drain path is blocked, most often the filter, then the drain hose, air gap, or disposal knockout plug. E25 points specifically at the drain pump: the pump cover is loose or not seated, or the impeller is jammed by a foreign object like a bit of glass or a fruit sticker. For E25, Bosch's guidance is to unclip the white pump cover and clear any debris, then check that the impeller can turn freely; if it can't, the pump is failing and needs replacement."
  },
  {
    q: "Is E15 the same as E24 or E25?",
    a: "No — and this is the one to get right. E15 is a leak code, not a drain code: water has collected in the base pan and tripped the AquaStop safety float, which shuts off the water intake. E24 and E25 mean water can't get out (a drain problem). They have different causes and different fixes. If you're seeing E15, see our Bosch E15 error page instead; if the tub is holding water with an E24 or E25, you're in the right place."
  },
  {
    q: "How do I check the drain pump on a Bosch dishwasher?",
    a: "This is one Bosch actually walks owners through for E24/E25. Power the unit off, remove the lower rack, and twist out the filter. Under it you'll see the white drain-pump cover — pop the clip (a teaspoon works) and lift the cover off. Clear any visible debris, then, wearing a glove, check that the impeller turns freely. If it spins easily and the filter and hose are clear, run a rinse cycle. If the impeller is stuck or won't turn freely after clearing debris, the drain pump is faulty and needs a technician to replace it. Do not attempt any electrical work."
  },
  {
    q: "How do I clear a Bosch dishwasher that won't drain?",
    a: "Safe, in order: scoop out the standing water, twist out and rinse the cylindrical filter (Bosch's #1 step) and clear the sump, unclip the white pump cover and clear debris and check the impeller spins freely, check the drain hose behind the unit for a kink and confirm the high loop, run the garbage disposal (a full one blocks the shared drain, and a new disposal may still have its knockout plug), and clear the sink air gap. Use only dishwasher detergent. Re-run a rinse cycle. If E24/E25 returns with everything clear, the pump needs replacing — that's our job."
  }
];

